<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahavamsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 5th–6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahavamsa, the &quot;Great Chronicle&quot;, is a Pali chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> composed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavihara">Mahavihara</a> monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, traditionally by the monk Mahanama, around the 5th or 6th century CE. It narrates the island's history from the legendary arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-vijaya">Prince Vijaya</a> to the reign of Mahasena in the 4th century, with Buddhist kingship as its central theme. Continued in later sections known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culavamsa">Culavamsa</a>, it is the main narrative source for early Sri Lankan history.</p></div>
